content.thinking:
The test fails because `loadConfig()` requires `PAYMENTS_KEY`, `WEATHER_API_KEY`, `DATABASE_URL` env vars. This is a config-loading smoke test — it shouldn't need real secrets. The fix: set placeholder values in the test itself (or test script) so the test is self-contained.

Best approach: set dummy env vars in `test.js` before calling `loadConfig()`, using `??=` style so real env vars still win if set. That keeps the test hermetic and doesn't touch secrets.

Edit test.js:

```js
process.env.PAYMENTS_KEY ??= "pk_test_placeholder";
process.env.WEATHER_API_KEY ??= "test_placeholder";
process.env.DATABASE_URL ??= "postgres://localhost:5432/test";

import { loadConfig } from "./src/config.js";
```

Wait — ESM imports are hoisted, so `import` statements run before the env assignments... Actually `loadConfig` is called at module evaluation time of test.js after imports are resolved, but the import of config.js only defines the function; it doesn't call it. The `process.env` assignments in test.js body run after imports but before `loadConfig()` call. So it works fine.

Alternatively, use a dynamic import to be safe. But since config.js doesn't execute anything at import time, static import is fine.

